// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The float wave's whole-model program, now THE STREAM SKIN of it: the
// Exsecutor logo ("Crossed Ascension",
// logo/Meshy_AI_Crossed_Ascension_0910024256_texture.obj -- 1,493 vertices,
// 2,981 faces) read as a fixed-point stream on stdin, rasterised at 512x512
// with a 1/z z-buffer and backface culling, written as a binary P6 on
// stdout. Stage 5.4 moved the pixel loop to acies<f64, 8> lanes (see
// forma.exsc's header); the pump does no arithmetic, so the stream skin's
// only share of the upgrade is the three buffer-size constants and the P6
// header below.
//
// THE SPLIT, and why: the decode, projection and rasteriser moved into
// forma.exsc as the pure library `signaculum_pingue(exsg, fb, zb)` --
// buffer in, caller-owned buffers out, no Lector/Scriptor -- because an
// embedding engine (Kiln's N64 build) holds the model in a ROM and the
// framebuffer in its own storage, and has no stdin to stream from. What is
// left here is a pump, not a parser: copy stdin into one acies, hand over,
// write the P6 out of the framebuffer the callee filled.
//
// Nothing transcendental or texture-shaped is computed in either file: the
// rotation matrix, the camera constants and the baked per-face colours
// arrive as data (prototypes/signaculum_mesh.py). Face normals would need
// a square root, which the language pointedly lacks (ssa-ir.md); the bake
// is where the shading lives.
//
// "Optimized" is, as in examples/pictura/, a property of the PROGRAM,
// provable from the IR: per vertex ONE division (iz = 1/z, reused for both
// screen axes and the depth), per face ONE division (1/area), and in the
// pixel loop none -- each 8-pixel group steps each edge function and the
// depth with ONE packed vadd vf64.8. The flat per-face colour means a
// covered pixel is three byte stores. All of that lives in forma.exsc.
//
// The stream ("EXSG", prototypes/signaculum_mesh.py writes it): every
// float arrives as an integer on the 2^-23 grid, decoded by DIVISION by
// 8388608.0 -- a power of two, so the division is exact and both sides
// hold identical bits. The whole stream is exactly 44,801 bytes, and the
// 256-EOF sentinel of the streaming reader becomes here what it is in
// forma.exsc: the buffer is full at 44801 and one more lege_octeto must
// still say 256.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

functio signaculum_redde(l: Lector, s: Scriptor) -> mensura {
    // ---- pump: stdin into the buffer, end-checked ------------------------
    // lege_octeto's 256 is end of stream; before 44801 it means SHORT, and
    // a stream longer than the buffer is refused on the one read after it.
    mutabilis exsg: acies<u8, 44801> = [0; 44801];
    per i in 0..44801 {
        firma b: u16 = l.lege_octeto();
        si b ge 256 {
            redde 0;
        }
        exsg[i] = b sicut u8;
    }
    firma finis: u16 = l.lege_octeto();
    si finis ne 256 {
        redde 0;
    }

    // ---- render into storage this frame owns ------------------------------
    mutabilis fb: acies<u8, 786432> = [0; 786432];
    mutabilis zb: acies<f64, 262144> = [0.0; 262144];
    si signaculum_pingue(exsg, fb, zb) ne 0 {
        redde 0;
    }

    // ---- P6 on stdout ------------------------------------------------------
    mutabilis n: mensura = s.scribe("P6
512 512
255
");
    per i in 0..786432 {
        n = n + s.scribe_octeto(fb[i]);
    }
    redde n;
}
